---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import PermutationCombination from '@/components/tools/calc2/PermutationCombination.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="permutation-and-combination-calculator">
  <PermutationCombination client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How the permutation and combination calculator works</h2>
    <p>
      Enter how many items there are (n) and how many you pick (r), then answer two questions: does the
      order matter, and can the same item be picked twice? The permutation and combination calculator gives
      the matching count as an exact whole number and shows the other three counts for comparison.
    </p>
    <pre><code>                      order matters        order does not matter
no repetition         nPr = n! ÷ (n − r)!  nCr = n! ÷ (r! (n − r)!)
with repetition       nʳ                   C(n + r − 1, r)</code></pre>
    <p>
      For small cases the animation puts each arrangement into the slots in turn and counts them, so you
      can see why ABC and BAC are two permutations but one combination.
    </p>

    <h2>Worked examples</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Gold, silver and bronze among 10 runners: order matters, no repetition. 10P3 = 10 × 9 × 8 = 720.</li>
      <li>A committee of 3 from 10 people: order does not matter. 10C3 = 720 ÷ 3! = 720 ÷ 6 = 120.</li>
      <li>A 4-digit PIN: order matters and digits can repeat. 10⁴ = 10,000.</li>
      <li>3 scoops from 5 flavours, repeats allowed, order ignored: C(5 + 3 − 1, 3) = C(7, 3) = 35.</li>
      <li>Poker hands: 52C5 = 2,598,960.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Why combinations are fewer</h2>
    <p>
      Every selection of r items can be put in order in r! ways. So nPr = nCr × r!, and nCr = nPr ÷ r!. For
      r = 3 that is a factor of 6: each committee of three corresponds to six podium orders.
    </p>

    <h2>Limits</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>n up to 100,000 and r up to 10,000. The results are exact; very long numbers are shown in scientific notation and copied in full.</li>
      <li>The animation is drawn for n up to 10 and r up to 6, and plays the first 400 arrangements.</li>
      <li>All n items are taken as different. Arrangements of letters with repeats, such as the word LETTER, need a different formula: n! divided by the factorial of each repeat count.</li>
    </ul>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
